=YX=5/3. The whole area is 3H/2, and each removed trapezoid has area (1+5/3)(H/3)/2=4H/9. The ratio is (3H/2-8H/9)/(3H/2)=11/27.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_269"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_268","cnm_139","cnm_p149"],"id":"parallel-widths-and-trapezoids","label":"Subtract the two end trapezoids","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-widths-and-trapezoids":["cnm_269","cnm_p150","cnm_266"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_269","cnm_p150","cnm_266","cnm_268","cnm_139","cnm_p149"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:0183b589b25939b15f769b22"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 40/3 inches","reason":"The bisector and parallelism give angle BAE=angle AEB, so BE=AB=8. Also CE/CB=DE/AB=5/8, hence CE/BE=5/3. Therefore CE=40/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_269"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1968_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_063","cnm_268","cnm_055"],"id":"bisector-and-parallel-cut","label":"Combine an isosceles triangle with a parallel cut","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bisector-and-parallel-cut":["cnm_269","cnm_058","cnm_138"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_269","cnm_058","cnm_138","cnm_069","cnm_063","cnm_268","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:0183b589b25939b15f769b22"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 2a+b","reason":"For X interior to BC, put t=BX/BC. Stewart's identity gives AX²=(1-t)AB²+tAC²-t(1-t)BC²<max(AB²,AC²). Therefore AA'<b, BB'<a, and CC'<a, so s<2a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_269"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"triangle-inequality-and-parallels","label":"Triangle inequality and parallels","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-inequality-and-parallels":["cnm_111","cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_111","cnm_269"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_268","cnm_110","cnm_096"],"id":"parallel-triangle-inequality-bound","label":"Use a parallel and the triangle inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-triangle-inequality-bound":["cnm_111","cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_111","cnm_269","cnm_268","cnm_110","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:0183b589b25939b15f769b22"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 4/11","reason":"AT/AD = 3/4 and ET/EB = 1/5. Writing S = [ABC], the height ratio gives [ATC] = S/5. Thus [ADC] = (4/3)[ATC] = 4S/15, so CD/BC = 4/15 and CD/BD = (4/15)/(11/15) = 4/11.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_269"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-section-ratios","label":"Parallel sections","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-section-ratios":["cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_269"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:0183b589b25939b15f769b22"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3:1","reason":"Let AD be parallel to CP with D on BC. The bisector and parallel-angle relations give CD=CA. Taking CA=3t and CB=4t gives BD=t, so BA/BP=BD/BC=1/4. Therefore PA=BP-BA=3AB.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_269"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-bisector-auxiliary","label":"Construct a parallel and use proportional segments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-bisector-auxiliary":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_269"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_055","cnm_268","cnm_067"],"id":"parallel-bisector-construction","label":"Draw a parallel to the bisector","methodDirect